<td id="S4.F3.3.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.12105v1/images/qualitative/dog_support.jpg" id="S4.F3.g6" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:91/91;" width="91" height="91" alt="Refer to caption"></td>
<td id="S4.F3.3.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.12105v1/images/qualitative/dog_query.png" id="S4.F3.g7" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:91/91;" width="91" height="91" alt="Refer to caption"></td>
<td id="S4.F3.3.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.12105v1/images/qualitative/dog_VRPSAM.png" id="S4.F3.g8" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:91/91;" width="91" height="91" alt="Refer to caption"></td>
<td id="S4.F3.3.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.12105v1/images/qualitative/dog_SAM2.png" id="S4.F3.g9" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:91/91;" width="91" height="91" alt="Refer to caption"></td>
<td id="S4.F3.3.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.12105v1/images/qualitative/dog_FSSAM2.png" id="S4.F3.g10" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:91/91;" width="91" height="91" alt="Refer to caption"></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Qualitative comparison results on the COCO-<math id="S4.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>20</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> dataset.
Our meta-training strategy successfully adapts SAM2, outperforming baseline methods.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present performance comparisons of FS-SAM2 against other state-of-the-art (SOTA) methods for a comprehensive evaluation.
To further illustrate the improvements of our method, we also compare with VRP-SAM2, which is our implementation of VRP-SAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> using SAM2 as the backbone instead of SAM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.3 Achieved Results ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ FS-SAM2: Adapting Segment Anything Model 2 for Few-Shot Semantic Segmentation via Low-Rank Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents performance comparisons on the PASCAL-<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>5</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> dataset.
In the <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-shot scenario, our model achieves a new SOTA mIoU of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="73.4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>73.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">73.4\%</annotation></semantics></math>, surpassing VRP-SAM by <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.5\%</annotation></semantics></math>, the most comparable method to ours.
The VRP-SAM method does not benefit from the newer SAM2 backbone, as it likely requires some fine-tuning of the backbone for the few-shot task.
It is worth noting that GF-SAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, which utilizes a DINOv2-L <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> backbone pre-trained on a dataset containing PASCAL-VOC, still underperforms compared to our method in the <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-shot setting.
We observe a slight improvement in segmentation performance in the <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>-shot setting, indicating that our method can leverage contextual information from multiple support images.
However, further gains could be achieved by explicitly training the model in a <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>-shot regime.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.3 Achieved Results ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ FS-SAM2: Adapting Segment Anything Model 2 for Few-Shot Semantic Segmentation via Low-Rank Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows results on the COCO-<math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>20</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> dataset and demonstrates the effectiveness and generalization capabilities of FS-SAM2.
Our model outperforms VRP-SAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> by <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.4\%</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-shot benchmark and shows a notable increase in the <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>-shot benchmark.
While GF-SAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> is the current SOTA on this benchmark, we note that it does not undergo meta-training for in-domain knowledge but instead relies on DINOv2 pre-training and is significantly more resource-intensive than FS-SAM2, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ FS-SAM2: Adapting Segment Anything Model 2 for Few-Shot Semantic Segmentation via Low-Rank Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We also evaluate our method on the FSS-<math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1000</annotation></semantics></math> dataset, that contains a large number of different classes, and on the domain-shift scenario, where the base training data and the testing data have a significant domain gap.
The results presented in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.3 Achieved Results ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ FS-SAM2: Adapting Segment Anything Model 2 for Few-Shot Semantic Segmentation via Low-Rank Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> clearly show the robustness of FS-SAM2 and demonstrate that LoRA successfully adapted the model to the few-shot setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.3 Achieved Results ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ FS-SAM2: Adapting Segment Anything Model 2 for Few-Shot Semantic Segmentation via Low-Rank Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> presents a qualitative comparison of our approach with VRP-SAM and the SAM2 without meta-training.
The examples highlight how the SAM2 struggles with dissimilar support-query pairs.
In contrast, FS-SAM2 consistently generates accurate and complete masks, capturing fine details and entire object regions that VRP-SAM misses.
Our method often generates cleaner boundaries, even refining minor inaccuracies present in the ground truth (GT) annotations.</p>
</div>
</section>
